# openOODA Language Specification (Tier A)
File: openOODA/spec.oot

This document is the authoritative Tier A language specification sheet for
openOODA. It specifies irreducible mechanical invariants, syntax grammar,
type algebra, capability security, memory physics, and wire protocols.
Normative modules reside in openOODA/docs/spec/. Target vision is in
openOODA/northstar.oot.

## 1. Specification Architecture Hierarchy

1. Tier A (This Sheet): High-density invariant specification sheet for AI
   coding agents, tools, and human engineers.
2. Tier B (Normative Codex): Formal 8-module specification suite in
   openOODA/docs/spec/ (01_lexical.oot through 08_runtime_abi.oot).
3. Tier C (Executable Substrate): Compiler (oodac), runtime (oodar), and
   standard library (std).
4. Non-Normative Vision: Conceptual architecture in openOODA/northstar.oot.

## 2. Irreducible Mechanical Invariants

1. Pure File Laws: Source code uses .oo. Governance and specifications use
   .oot. Strictly prohibited: .py, .js, .ts, .rb, .pl, and .sh outside
   authorized slots.
2. File Line Ceiling: Every .oo and .oot file contains a maximum of 256
   lines.
3. Academy 4-Element Header: Every product .oo file begins with:
   // # <Title>, // Logline:, // Setup:, // Beats:.
4. Zero Ambient Authority: Code receives authority only through caller
   arguments. Privileged operations require an unforgeable capability token
   reference (&Cap).
5. No Parentheses on Conditions: Condition expressions in if and while
   blocks forbid outer enclosing parentheses. Use "if cond {" and
   "while cond {".
6. Zero Struct Trailing Commas: Struct declarations and struct literals
   forbid a trailing comma before the closing brace "}".
7. Flat Relative Imports: Module imports use flat relative string paths
   (import "module.oo";). Double-colon namespace qualifiers (::) are
   strictly prohibited.

## 3. Keyword Partitioning

### 3.1 Active Normative Keywords (22)
The active compiler and runtime recognize exactly 22 keywords:
fn, let, pub, mut, import, requires, ensures, verify, if, else, match, for,
in, while, return, type, true, false, gpu, break, continue, export.

### 3.2 Residual Non-Normative Keywords (5)
The following 5 keywords are reserved for future architectural vision:
trait, newtype, where, spec, impl.
Residual keywords are non-normative. The active parser and type checker reject
them. Language servers suppress them from code completion.

## 4. Type System & Algebraic Data Types

### 4.1 Scalar Primitives
- Int: Signed 64-bit two's complement integer (-2^63 to 2^63 - 1).
- Float: 64-bit IEEE 754 double precision floating point number.
- Bool: Boolean truth value (true or false).
- String: Immutable UTF-8 encoded byte sequence view.
- Char: 32-bit Unicode scalar value.
- Byte: Unsigned 8-bit integer (0 to 255).
- Unit: The empty tuple () representing absence of value.

### 4.2 Compound Types
- Structs: Fixed-layout records without trailing commas:
  type Point = struct { x: Int, y: Int }
- Tagged Unions (Sum Types): Disjoint algebraic variants:
  type Shape = Circle(Float) | Rect(Float, Float);
  Represented in memory as a 32-bit integer tag followed by payload data.
- Collections: List[T] (dynamic growable array), Map[K, V] (hash map).

### 4.3 Must-Use Error Handling
- Result[T, E] = Ok(T) | Err(E);
- Option[T] = Some(T) | None;
- The compiler enforces must-use semantics. Discarding a Result or Option
  without unwrapping through match or ? raises a compile-time error.

### 4.4 Reference Semantics
- Shared immutable reference: &T.
- Mutable reference: &mut T.
- Capability reference: &Cap.

## 5. Capability Tokens & Security Lattice

### 5.1 The 20 Normative Language Capability Tokens
All side-effect operations require one of 20 unforgeable capability tokens:
1.  FsReadCap: Read files and list filesystem directories.
2.  FsWriteCap: Write and modify filesystem files.
3.  FsCap: Umbrella filesystem token subsuming read and write.
4.  ProcessCap: Spawn and control operating system subprocesses.
5.  SysCap: Execute low-level operating system controls and termination.
6.  NetCap: Umbrella network token subsuming TCP, UDP, and BIND.
7.  TcpCap: Open, read, and write TCP network stream sockets.
8.  UdpCap: Send and receive UDP network datagram packets.
9.  BindCap: Bind network sockets to local ports and listen.
10. EnvCap: Read allowed process environment variables (OODA_*).
11. TimeCap: Read system clock, wall time, and timer intervals.
12. RandCap: Generate cryptographically secure random bytes.
13. AllocCap: Allocate dynamic heap memory within process quotas.
14. ArenaCap: Allocate memory from contiguous linear bump arenas.
15. ThreadCap: Spawn parallel actor threads with isolated heaps.
16. GpuCap: Dispatch compute kernels to GPU hardware accelerators.
17. FfiCap: Call external C foreign function interface symbols.
18. MetricsCap: Read process telemetry counters and metrics.
19. SignCap: Sign and verify capability HMAC tokens.
20. CompilerReadCap: Query compiler and language server symbol tables.

### 5.2 Substrate Capabilities (26 Bits)
The runtime C substrate defines 26 capability bit positions in caps.h:
- Active language tokens: 20 capability bits.
- Reserved device bits (6): Audio (20), Camera (21), Usb (22), Hid (23),
  Window (24), Frame (25).
- Dead excised bits: 13 (HITL), 14 (SYNC), 15 (MEM), 16 (HTTP).
  Excised bits are permanently retired and must not be reallocated.

### 5.3 Attenuation Algebra
- Subsumption: FsCap satisfies FsReadCap and FsWriteCap. NetCap satisfies
  TcpCap, UdpCap, and BindCap.
- Subset Rule: A child capability must be a subset of the parent capability:
  (parent & child) == child.
- Cryptographic Sealing: Attenuated tokens use HMAC-SHA256 parent signatures.
- Path Scoping: OoPathCap restricts FsReadCap to designated path prefixes.
- TLS Attenuation: TlsCap attenuates from TcpCap with verified credentials.

## 6. Memory Physics & Concurrency

### 6.1 Linear Bump Arenas
- Contiguous virtual memory regions for scratch allocations.
- Allocation increments a linear offset pointer.
- O(1) bulk resets via checkpoints (oo_checkpoint) and rollbacks (oo_rollback).
- Zero garbage collection pauses and zero memory fragmentation.

### 6.2 Automatic Reference Counting (ARC)
- Deterministic deallocation for strings, lists, and heap records at zero
  reference count.
- Cycles are forbidden in purely algebraic immutable values.
- Scope exit unwinds lexical blocks to release allocations on early return,
  break, or continue.

### 6.3 Actor Concurrency
- Share-nothing actor concurrency model.
- Each actor thread possesses an isolated memory arena and heap.
- Actors communicate exclusively through typed, synchronized message channels.

## 7. Machine Wire Protocol & Diagnostics

### 7.1 TSV Diagnostic Protocol
- Diagnostic success message: OK\t<verb>\t<message>
- Diagnostic error message: ERR\t<category>\t<message>
- Standard error categories: lex, parse, type, cap, contract, import.

### 7.2 Token Dump Protocol
oodac tokens <file> emits tab-separated token streams:
<KIND>\t<LINE>\t<COL>\t<TEXT>
The stream terminates with:
EOF\t<line>\t<col>\t

### 7.3 Process Exit Code Matrix
- 0: Success (clean build, type check, test execution, or command pass).
- 1: Execution failure (test failure, runtime abort, contract violation).
- 2: Invocation error (invalid CLI flags, bad arguments, syntax error).
- 3: Capability violation (E_CAP trap, unauthorized resource access).

### 7.4 Blackbox Crash Autopsy
Fatal signals (SIGSEGV, SIGABRT) write an autopsy file to
.blackbox/autopsy.json containing failure category, coordinate, stack
trace, and root cause without dynamic heap allocation.
